  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1256 AM MST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nSat img shows high amplitude trough nearing the west coast, with\na baroclinic cloud leaf already over southern Idaho. Thus,\nshould get plenty of moisture in the warm sector starting likely\nby this afternoon in the mountains and highlands and later for\nthe lower elevations as the pattern shifts eastward. As the cold\nfront approaches, precipitation continues and the colder air\ndoes, by Tue afternoon, produce snow as low as Snake River plain\nelevations. The Advisory level accumulations are still fairly\nhigh in elevation at this time; Stanley at 6200ft elevation\nreceives 4 inches, while Hailey likely to only get a trace at\n5500ft elevation. Heavier amounts but still at high elevation in\nthe southern highlands, while the eastern highlands barely get\nto Advisory level. In the Snake River plain, from Pocatello\nnorthward 1 to 3 inches of snow with highest amounts on the\nnorth end well north of even St Anthony, with most at 1 to 2.5\ninches of snow accumulation. Any valley locations west or south\nof Pocatello Airport will likely see rain only.\n\nModerate to windy conditions arrive in the cold sector, so it\nholds off until Tue afternoon/Tue evening before gusts to 30 mph\ndevelop. By Late Tue night possibly a small area from Idaho \nFalls eastward could see Wind Advisory.",
